		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Contact force dependent friction calculation]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=32229#p32229"><![CDATA[
Most physics engine I know, including Bullet, use the normal force for friction, approximated as a friction pyramid. When using an iterative solver such as PGS you can re-compute the maximum friction forces at each PGS iteration. <br><br>It is also possible to formulate the friction as part of the MLCP, see function (54) in our Eurographics state of the art paper (<a href="https://code.google.com/p/bullet/downloads/detail?name=STAR.pdf" class="postlink">https://code.google.com/p/bullet/downlo ... e=STAR.pdf</a>) . The resulting LCP cannot be solved using PGS, so you need an MLCP solver such as Lemke or PATH. You can see an example implementation of this friction model here: <a href="https://github.com/erwincoumans/daVincyCode3D/blob/master/dVC3d_Bullet/src/BulletDynamics/Dynamics/btST2World.cpp#L330" class="postlink">https://github.com/erwincoumans/daVincy ... d.cpp#L330</a><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Sun Nov 24, 2013 3:06 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
